But their auto generated API clients don&#x27;t have the invariant baked into them, which makes them very rough to use in say Rust.<p>They&#x27;ll have something like this:<p><pre><code>    MySchema:
      type: object
      properties:
        enum_member:   { enum: [foo, bar] }
        enum_member_2: { enum: [quz, qux] }
      if:   { properties: { enum_member: { const: bar } } }
      then: { required: [enum_member_2] }
</code></pre>
Except, this is not defined in schemas, this is merely defined in prose around the documentation.<p>So your Rust API is full of Option, and it gets really annoying when you&#x27;re testing things like say Bedrock, which takes 10 minutes, and then fails because what you submitted 10 minutes before doesn&#x27;t match their schema.<p>I understand this can be fixed by reading the documentation in more detail, but it has gotten so verbose, and sometimes you just want to start something.
